If you're working from home full-time, sitting for 8 to 10 hours straight isn't just uncomfortable — it's bad for your back, your circulation, and your long-term health. A full electric standing desk is great, but if you already have a desk you like (or you're renting and can't swap furniture), a standing desk converter gives you the sit-stand flexibility without replacing your entire setup. We tested three of the most popular options on the market to help you decide.

The VariDesk Pro Plus 36 is the category heavyweight — literally. It weighs about 52 pounds and sits on top of your existing desk. The build quality is immediately obvious: the steel frame feels industrial-grade, and the two-tier design gives you an upper tier for your monitors and a lower keyboard tray. What sets the VariDesk apart is its spring-assisted lift mechanism. You squeeze two paddles on either side and the desk rises smoothly with almost no effort, even with two monitors and a laptop loaded on top. There are 11 height settings, and the whole thing locks firmly into place at each one without wobble. The 36-inch model fits dual monitors comfortably. At around $395, it's the most expensive option here, but it's the one that feels like it'll outlast your career. The downside: it's heavy and not something you'll move around often. Assembly is minimal — you basically lift it out of the box and set it on your desk.

The FlexiSpot M2B is the budget-friendly alternative that doesn't feel cheap. It uses a gas spring lift mechanism that, unlike the VariDesk's dual-paddle system, operates with a single lever on the right side. It raises and lowers smoothly, though you'll need a bit more muscle if your monitors are heavy. The M2B is a single-tier design — your keyboard and monitor sit on the same surface, so both rise and fall together. This makes it more compact (35 inches wide) and lighter (about 32 pounds), but it means you lose the separate keyboard tray that keeps your wrists at an ergonomic angle when standing. The surface is a textured black laminate that resists scratches well. At around $170, it's less than half the price of the VariDesk and still delivers a solid sit-stand experience. Assembly takes about 15 minutes with the included hex key.

The VIVO Height Adjustable Standing Desk Converter is the ultra-budget pick at around $130. It's also a single-tier design at 36 inches wide. The lift mechanism is pneumatic, and while it works, it's noticeably less smooth than the FlexiSpot or VariDesk — you'll feel some resistance and occasional jerkiness, especially with heavier loads. The keyboard tray is integrated into the single platform rather than being a separate tier, which means your typing height is tied to your monitor height. For shorter users this can work fine, but if you're over 5'10", you may find the monitor sits a bit low when the keyboard is at a comfortable standing height. Build quality is decent with a powder-coated steel frame, but the laminate surface shows wear faster than the FlexiSpot. It's a good entry-level option if you want to try the standing desk lifestyle without a big investment, but it won't impress you.

After using all three for a week each, here's our verdict: the VariDesk Pro Plus 36 is the best overall if budget allows. The dual-tier design, effortless lift, and rock-solid stability make it a genuine pleasure to use every day. The FlexiSpot M2B is the best value — it gives you 85% of the VariDesk experience at less than half the price, and for most remote workers, it's the sweet spot. The VIVO is a capable starter unit, but the less refined lift mechanism and single-tier limitations mean you'll probably want to upgrade within a year if standing becomes a regular part of your routine.

One tip regardless of which you choose: get an anti-fatigue mat. Standing on a hard floor for hours will make your feet and knees ache no matter how good your converter is. A $30 mat makes standing sessions genuinely comfortable instead of something you endure between sitting stretches.

